1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,285
A do-not-call list holds the phone numbers your company must not call.

2
00:00:05,285 --> 00:00:09,458
This video shows why the lists exist, where they are checked, how to

3
00:00:09,458 --> 00:00:13,518
upload your own list, how to make every import check them, and how

4
00:00:13,518 --> 00:00:15,830
to connect the national registries.

5
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:24,886
Laws in many countries forbid calling people who asked not to be called,

6
00:00:24,886 --> 00:00:27,800
and the fines for a single call can be large.

7
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:31,015
The numbers come from several places:

8
00:00:31,015 --> 00:00:35,659
your own suppression list of customers who said no, files you download each

9
00:00:35,659 --> 00:00:38,010
month, and the national registries.

10
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:43,468
A purchased list or a spreadsheet from another system can hold hundreds of

11
00:00:43,468 --> 00:00:46,940
such numbers, and no agent can check them one by one.

12
00:00:47,690 --> 00:00:51,922
So you keep the lists in one place, and the site checks them for you:

13
00:00:51,922 --> 00:00:56,210
when contacts are imported, and again before the dialer places a call.

14
00:01:00,410 --> 00:01:05,954
When you import contacts or leads, the file can be checked against the lists you choose.

15
00:01:05,954 --> 00:01:10,200
Your administrator can make some lists mandatory for every import.

16
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:16,658
A dialer profile that has Respect do-not-call and communication preferences turned on checks

17
00:01:16,658 --> 00:01:19,978
every connected list and registry before each call.

18
00:01:19,978 --> 00:01:22,250
A number on a list is not called.

19
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,805
Depending on how your site is set up, calls that agents dial themselves

20
00:01:26,805 --> 00:01:28,040
can be checked too.

21
00:01:28,790 --> 00:01:35,302
A registry that cannot answer, for example because it is unreachable, never counts as not listed.

22
00:01:35,302 --> 00:01:39,664
An import skips that row and says why, and the dialer tries the

23
00:01:39,664 --> 00:01:41,940
record again in a later cycle.

24
00:01:46,210 --> 00:01:49,627
Open Interaction Center, Local DNC Registry.

25
00:01:49,627 --> 00:01:52,702
It keeps the lists you upload yourself.

26
00:01:52,702 --> 00:01:56,530
Nothing is uploaded yet, so click Upload new list.

27
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:03,703
Enter a List name that says what the list is, for example June 2026 DNC Update.

28
00:02:04,201 --> 00:02:06,769
Pick the Country the numbers belong to.

29
00:02:06,769 --> 00:02:11,218
The menu shows each country's calling code, and numbers in the file that

30
00:02:11,218 --> 00:02:14,641
have no plus sign are read as numbers of this country.

31
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:18,630
Choose the CSV file, and click Upload.

32
00:02:25,876 --> 00:02:30,310
The page returns right away and the list imports in the background.

33
00:02:30,310 --> 00:02:35,596
Its row shows how many records were processed while it runs, and then its result.

34
00:02:39,796 --> 00:02:44,881
The file is a CSV with one phone number on each row, in a single column.

35
00:02:44,881 --> 00:02:49,772
If your list is a spreadsheet with several columns or tabs, save just

36
00:02:49,772 --> 00:02:52,706
the phone number column as a CSV first.

37
00:02:53,456 --> 00:02:56,626
A header row such as Phone Number is optional.

38
00:02:56,626 --> 00:02:59,796
It is skipped, and is not counted as an error.

39
00:03:00,546 --> 00:03:05,372
Every number is converted to its full international form before it is stored.

40
00:03:05,372 --> 00:03:08,396
Numbers that cannot be converted are rejected.

41
00:03:09,146 --> 00:03:14,305
Blank rows, rows with more than one value, and repeated numbers are skipped

42
00:03:14,305 --> 00:03:16,726
and counted as rejected records.

43
00:03:20,935 --> 00:03:26,413
Each list shows its file name, country, status, how many records were imported,

44
00:03:26,413 --> 00:03:30,345
how many were rejected, and when it was last processed.

45
00:03:30,889 --> 00:03:33,791
Completed means every row was imported.

46
00:03:33,791 --> 00:03:39,013
Completed with errors means the list finished, but some rows were rejected.

47
00:03:39,013 --> 00:03:44,429
A list that is still importing, paused or failed is not used for checking yet.

48
00:03:44,882 --> 00:03:47,442
Open the Actions menu of a list.

49
00:03:47,442 --> 00:03:53,572
Download errors gives you the rejected rows, with the reason for each, as a CSV file.

50
00:03:54,030 --> 00:03:58,873
A list that is pending, paused, failed or importing has more actions.

51
00:03:58,873 --> 00:04:04,362
Process now starts or continues a list without waiting for the background task,

52
00:04:04,362 --> 00:04:07,850
and Pause import stops a list that is importing.

53
00:04:08,312 --> 00:04:12,532
Delete removes the list and all its phone numbers in the background.

54
00:04:13,014 --> 00:04:17,366
To replace a list each month, upload the new file first, wait until

55
00:04:17,366 --> 00:04:20,467
it is completed, and then delete the old list.

56
00:04:20,467 --> 00:04:24,104
That way your numbers are never unprotected in between.

57
00:04:28,358 --> 00:04:33,789
Use this when company policy says every contact import must be checked.

58
00:04:33,789 --> 00:04:36,328
Open Settings, Content Import.

59
00:04:36,785 --> 00:04:40,053
Tick Enforce do-not-call registry checks globally.

60
00:04:40,053 --> 00:04:46,005
Every contact import must then check phone numbers, and the person importing cannot turn it off.

61
00:04:46,572 --> 00:04:51,651
Under Enforced registries, tick the lists and registries that are always checked.

62
00:04:51,651 --> 00:04:55,912
People can add more when they import, but they cannot remove these.

63
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:58,290
Click Save.

64
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:06,580
Open Content, Import, and start an import of contacts.

65
00:05:06,580 --> 00:05:10,770
The Do-not-call registry section is on the import screen.

66
00:05:14,257 --> 00:05:18,436
An enforced list shows an Enforced badge, and the option reads that the

67
00:05:18,436 --> 00:05:21,747
setting is globally enforced by the site administrator.

68
00:05:22,337 --> 00:05:25,925
When a number is on a registry, Skip the row leaves it out

69
00:05:25,925 --> 00:05:28,784
of the import and lists it in the error file.

70
00:05:28,784 --> 00:05:32,652
Import it marked Do not call keeps the record, so the number is

71
00:05:32,652 --> 00:05:36,857
recognized if it is bought or imported again, and it is never called.

72
00:05:37,444 --> 00:05:43,811
Under Select registries to check against, add any other list or registry for this import.

73
00:05:43,811 --> 00:05:46,424
Choose the file and click Upload.

74
00:05:46,890 --> 00:05:49,120
The import runs in the background.

75
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:53,523
The Bulk Import page shows Completed with errors, because the rows on the

76
00:05:53,523 --> 00:05:57,090
list were left out and counted as errors, with the reason.

77
00:06:01,307 --> 00:06:06,100
Each national registry needs an account with the registry's operator.

78
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:12,107
Get the account details first, then open Settings, DNC Registries and pick the registry.

79
00:06:12,596 --> 00:06:20,326
For the USA FTC Registry, enter the Organization ID registered with the FTC for API access.

80
00:06:20,822 --> 00:06:24,910
Then the API key the FTC gave you, and the Base URL.

81
00:06:24,910 --> 00:06:29,702
Keep the suggested address unless the FTC tells you otherwise.

82
00:06:37,670 --> 00:06:41,686
The Canada LNNTE-DNCL Registry works the same way:

83
00:06:41,686 --> 00:06:47,207
enter the Account number registered with the CRTC, the API key, and the

84
00:06:47,207 --> 00:06:49,430
Base URL, and click Save.

85
00:06:57,092 --> 00:07:01,124
After you save an API key, the box stays empty and a note

86
00:07:01,124 --> 00:07:02,852
says a key is stored.

87
00:07:02,852 --> 00:07:07,652
Leave the box empty to keep the key, or type a new one to replace it.

88
00:07:11,852 --> 00:07:15,687
A company with its own suppression list uploads it as a local list,

89
00:07:15,687 --> 00:07:19,312
so the numbers of customers who said no are never called again.

90
00:07:20,062 --> 00:07:24,962
A team that buys leads makes the registries mandatory for every import, so

91
00:07:24,962 --> 00:07:28,392
a purchased file is cleaned before anyone sees it.

92
00:07:29,142 --> 00:07:33,534
A company that calls in two countries keeps one local list for each

93
00:07:33,534 --> 00:07:36,722
country, and connects both national registries.

94
00:07:37,472 --> 00:07:41,587
A monthly registry download is uploaded as a new list, and the old

95
00:07:41,587 --> 00:07:44,502
one is deleted once the new one is completed.

96
00:07:46,502 --> 00:07:50,556
Keep your lists in one place, make every import check them, and let

97
00:07:50,556 --> 00:07:52,999
the dialer skip what it must not call.

98
00:07:52,999 --> 00:07:54,442
Thanks for watching.

